## 2. Types and static analysis

- The strictest practical setting of the language's checker is on from the first commit (see Stack rules
  below for the exact flags). Loosening a flag needs an owner decision recorded in `docs/STACK.md`. [ci] [review]
- Boundary data (HTTP input, env, files, queues, third-party responses, JSON columns) is parsed into a
  typed value once, at the boundary; inside, nothing re-checks or casts it. [review]
- No `any`/`mixed`/`interface{}` escaping a module; no casts that silence the checker on data that crossed
  a boundary. [ci] [review]
- Enums for closed sets, with exhaustive matches; adding a case must break the build where it is unhandled. [ci]
- **Ratchets only shrink.** Baselines (type checker, linter, duplicate-code and suppression counts) exist to pay
  down legacy debt, never to get a change green. A change that adds an ignore, a baseline entry or a
  suppression comment without a named reason and a linked card is refused. [review] [not yet: a baseline-size guard per tool]

## 3. Queries, loops and data access

- **An N+1 fails in tests, not in production.** Where the ORM lazy-loads, lazy loading throws outside
  production (the exact switch is in Stack rules below); the query-count test below covers the rest. [guard-test]
- **Every list endpoint has a query-count test** at 1 row and at 20 rows; the count must not grow with
  rows. [guard-test]
- **Every query and loop is bounded**: pagination or `LIMIT` with a maximum page size for anything a user
  can grow; batch jobs walk by id in chunks; no unbounded `IN (...)` built from input; retries have a cap
  and backoff. [review]
- **Authorization on every state-changing route, proven by a test on that route.** Each POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE
  route (and every child route of a resource: `/orders/{id}/items/{item}` is its own route) has a test that
  a caller without the permission is refused. A test on the parent route does not cover the child. [guard-test: a route-table test fails when a state-changing route has no authorization marker]
- **A read that gates a write holds the lock.** "Check the balance, then debit" runs in one transaction
  with a row lock (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`) or is made safe by a unique constraint; idempotency keys on
  any write a client may retry. [review]{{#if money}} [guard-test: a concurrency test for every money-moving path]{{/if}}
- **Time zones are passed explicitly.** Never rely on the server or database default zone; store instants
  in UTC; convert at the edge with an explicit zone; date-only values are dates, not midnight timestamps. [review]
- **Malformed input fails closed.** A parse error, an unknown enum value, a missing signature or an
  unreadable config refuses the operation; it never falls back to "allow" or to a default that widens
  access. [review] [guard-test for auth and webhooks]
- **Pending is a distinct state.** An accepted-but-not-applied command, an unconfirmed payment, a device
  that has never connected: each has its own status value, separate from both "done" and "nothing there".
  "A row exists" is not "it works". [review]
- **No defensive guards around things that must exist.** If a value cannot be null by construction, do
  not add `?->`/`if (x)` around it: a guard that cannot fire hides the bug when the invariant breaks.
  Assert or let it throw. [review]
{{#if multiTenant}}
- **Tenant isolation is enforced twice**: the application scope on every tenant-owned query AND database
  row-level security with a deny-by-default predicate. A guard test fails when a tenant-owned table lacks
  either. A test per model proves a foreign tenant's row is invisible and unwritable. [guard-test]
{{/if}}

## 4. One fact, one derivation

- Two surfaces that show the same fact (a status badge on a list and on a detail page, a price in a
  checkout and on an invoice, a permission in the menu and in the policy) call ONE function. Import it;
  never restate it. [review]
- A comment saying "aligned with", "mirrors", "kept in sync with" or "same as" is a finding: it marks a copy
  that will drift. Replace the copy with the import. [review] [not yet: a grep gate for those phrases]
- Fixing one surface and not its twin is the same bug: search for every caller of the derivation before
  closing the card. [review]
- Copy-paste detection runs on every change; a new duplicated chunk fails the gate (the baseline only
  shrinks). [ci: dupes] (a stack with no `dupes` gate: [not yet: copy-paste gate])
- **Size budget:** a class or module over ~300 lines, or a function over ~50, needs a reason in review;
  split by responsibility, not by line count. [review] [not yet: a size-report gate]
- Reuse first: before adding a helper, component, endpoint or status formula, search for the existing one
  and extend it with a parameter. [review]

## 5. Errors, logs and secrets

- Errors and logs never leak exception messages, stack traces, SQL, bindings, tokens or personal data to
  users or to third-party log sinks. Users get a message key; logs get an error id, a class and safe
  context. [review] [guard-test: a test renders the 500 page and asserts no exception text]
- Secrets live in the environment or a secret store, never in the repo; `secrets` (gitleaks) runs in the
  release gate. [ci: secrets]
{{#if pii}}
- Personal data: collected only with a purpose, encrypted at rest where it is sensitive (IDs, biometrics,
  health), excluded from logs and analytics, exportable and deletable per person. [review]
{{/if}}
{{#if money}}
- Money: integer minor units plus a currency code; never floats. Every money movement is idempotent,
  audited, and reconciled against the provider. [review] [guard-test]
{{/if}}
- **Log every action that changes state** in an append-only audit log with who, what, when and the
  before/after, as locale-neutral `{key, params}`. [review] [not yet: a guard test that every Action writes an audit entry]

## 6. Tests

- A test exists to fail for its named reason. Before trusting a new test, break the code it covers and
  watch it fail with that reason. [review]
- **Assert the mechanism, not the class string**: assert that the refused user got a 403 and nothing was
  written, not that a CSS class or an error message string appears. [review]
- **No wall-clock or random dates.** Freeze time; build dates relative to the frozen now; seeds are
  fixed. A test that passes at 23:59 and fails at 00:01 is broken. [review]
- **Factories can produce the unsafe shape**: another tenant's row, a soft-deleted parent, an expired
  token, a cancelled subscription, as named states, so guards can be proven. [review]
- **The test database engine is the production engine** (Postgres in tests if Postgres in production;
  never SQLite as a stand-in). Isolation via transactions or a database per worker. [ci]
- Inner loop: affected tests only (`wf gates`); the full suite runs in `wf gates --release`. A flaky test
  is fixed or quarantined with a card the same day; a rerun is allowed only for a documented, evidenced
  cause and is named in the result. [ci] [review]
- Shared test resources (database, ports, dev server) go through locks, never `while pgrep` loops. [review]

## 7. Internationalization

{{#if i18n}}
Locales: {{LOCALES}}. Widest: `{{WIDEST_LOCALE}}` (every visual check runs in it).
{{/if}}
{{#unless i18n}}
Locales: {{LOCALES}}. One locale today, but every string is a key from day one, so adding a language is
data, not a rewrite.
{{/unless}}
- **Keys only.** No literal user-facing strings in code or templates. That includes `aria-label`, `title`,
  `alt`, `placeholder` and `sr-only` text, emails, PDF exports, and validation messages. [not yet: i18n check]
- **An enum is not a label.** A payload or audit entry never carries a raw enum as display text; send a
  key and let the reader resolve it. [review]
- **Plurals use ICU MessageFormat** (`{count, plural, one {...} other {...}}`), which knows every locale's
  plural rules; no `count === 1 ? a : b`. [review] [guard-test: ICU arguments match across locales]
- **Keys land in all locales together**, in the same change, with real translations or an explicit
  `needs-translation` marker that a gate reports. [guard-test]
- Instants stay machine-readable in payloads and audit entries; they are formatted at render time in the
  reader's zone and locale. [review]
- The destructive verb is lexically distinct from the dismiss verb in every locale (see UI/UX standards). [review]

## 8. Review

- Review depth scales with risk (`wf risk`): **light** (only `*.md`, `docs/`, `tests/` or `*.css` changed)
  gets the gates plus a manager spot check of the diff, no reviewer agent; **standard** at least
  `review.minPasses` (default 1) adversarial reviewer rounds; **high** (paths in `risk.high`: {{RISK_HIGH}},
  or the built-in high patterns when that list is empty) two independent reviewers at once plus a
  security pass{{#if ownerReviewsHigh}}, then the owner{{/if}}. [review]
- Fix commits are re-reviewed as their own diff; review rounds are capped (`review.maxRounds`), and the
  minor tail becomes cards. [review]
- Reviewers read with `git show`/`git diff`; read-only agents never check out, stash or reset. [review]
- Every review checks the rules tagged `[review]` in this file and in `docs/UI-UX-STANDARDS.md` that apply
  to the diff. [review]
