## 9. Agents and parallel work

- Implementation subagents work in **isolated git worktrees on disjoint file sets**, one card each. [review]
- The manager session integrates every branch and runs every gate; an agent's "tests pass" is not
  evidence until the manager reruns them. [review]
- Worktrees are made runnable by `wf wt-init` (real installs or copy-on-write, never symlinked bundler
  directories). [ci]
- Read-only agents never mutate the checkout (`checkout`, `stash`, `reset`, `switch`). [review]
- Agents never type gate commands by hand; they run `wf gates` and paste its table. [review]
- **The third repetition becomes a script.** When a sequence of commands is run a third time, it becomes
  a `wf` subcommand, a project script or a skill, and the docs point to it. [review]
- Versions come from `docs/STACK.md`'s version table (generated by `wf versions`), never from memory. A
  version not in that table is not written into code, config or docs. [review] `wf doctor` warns when the
  table is older than 90 days; `wf versions --check docs/STACK.md` re-verifies it against the registries.

## 10. Handoff discipline

- Every session that stops mid-work leaves the board, the card's `## Notes` and `wf handoff` current, so a
  fresh session can resume from the files alone. [review]
- Decisions the owner makes are recorded where the next reader looks (the card, `docs/STACK.md`, this
  file), dated, with the reason. [review]
- A handoff describes a dated snapshot; the next session re-checks branch, status and the live tracker
  before acting on it. [review]

{{STACK_STANDARDS}}

## Project-specific rules

<!-- tune: rules that only this product needs (domain invariants, regulatory constraints, hardware safety) -->
{{#if hardware}}
- Device control goes through a queued command channel with acknowledgements; "sent" is not "applied".
  Life-safety behaviour (fail-safe egress, interlocks) is enforced by the device, never gated by the
  software. A simulator run never replaces a check on the physical device. [review]
{{/if}}
{{#if offline}}
- Offline: the client queues writes with idempotency keys and shows each as pending until the server
  confirms; conflicts surface to the user, never resolve silently. [review]
{{/if}}
{{#if realtime}}
- Realtime: a pushed update is a hint to refetch, not the source of truth; the page is correct after a
  reload with no socket. Channels are authorized per subscriber. [review] [guard-test]
{{/if}}
